Nesting and branches
A nesting block (If, for example) has a vertical bar on its left; drag a block to the right of that bar and it becomes its child;
children can nest further, with no depth limit.

[b]If[/b] exports as one [code]jump[/code]: it [b]skips the whole nest when the condition does not hold[/b] — which is why the
comparison is written the other way round, so "If a equals b" exports [code]jump … notEqual a b[/code]. The choices are
equal / notEqual / lessThan / lessThanEq / greaterThan / greaterThanEq; strictEqual cannot be negated by a single jump,
so build it with [b]Jump[/b] yourself.
The exported line also ends with a marker ([code]#if a=1 cmp=equal b=2[/code]); import uses it to turn that line back into an If and put the nest back.

[b]While[/b] is a conditional loop and exports as [b]two[/b] [code]jump[/code]s: a head that skips the whole nest when the condition
does not hold, plus a tail jump back to the head ([code]jump 1 always 0 0[/code]) — the exit is after the tail.
It also carries a marker ([code]#while a=1 cmp=lessThan b=2[/code]), so it comes back as a While with its nest.

[b]Break[/b] / [b]Continue[/b] only make sense inside a loop nest: Break is one unconditional jump straight to just after the loop
([code]jump 5 always 0 0[/code]); Continue jumps to the loop's tail jump ([code]jump 3 always 0 0[/code]), i.e. back to the condition.
Both line numbers are computed at export time; outside a loop they export [code]-1[/code] with a console warning. The line ends with a
[code]#break[/code] / [code]#continue[/code] marker, so it comes back as the same block.
Break targets just after the loop — when the loop sits at the very end of the program, that is the trailing [code]stop[/code].


Use [b]Jump[/b] to jump somewhere else.